				"avatarUrl": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/avatars/original/missing.png"
			},
			"date": "2025-07-21T07:39:15.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Because software in this context usually means a highly complex system and as such it needs to fulfill a lot of requirements at once. Only a single purpose thing can be done exceptionaly good. With each additional requirement rises the probability of inevitable trade-offs that make the overal appearance \"not good\". Note that we often don't realize or acknowledge all the requirements on software that it needs to fulfill because they seem secondary or non-functional.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
